## 12. Conclusion

The threshold hypothesis is testable, the legal-certainty index is quantifiable, and the empirical data now exists to calibrate both. This revised proposal moves from a probability tracker to a **threshold model with an endogenized learning curve (\( \alpha = 0.32 \)), a legal multiplier with explicit functional-form hypotheses, a Bayesian network with DAG and CPT structure, and a model-comparison test against smooth alternatives**.

The dissertation does not assert that "the threshold framing is correct." It specifies what evidence would confirm or refute it: a threshold model that outperforms logistic and power-law alternatives on pre-registered out-of-sample predictions. The cross-domain "structural invariance" argument is presented as **motivation**, not evidence; the dissertation states what in the space case would have to be true for the same structure to apply.

The scope discipline is: **three downstream markets, one endogenous driver, one legal scenario parameter, one Bayesian network, one model-comparison test.** The full solar-system economy is the subject of the thesis, not the object of the model.

