## Appendix A — Learning-Curve Specification: Derivation, Calibration, and Sensitivity

### A.1 Wright's Original Specification

Wright (1936) observed that unit cost declines by a constant proportion with each doubling of cumulative production:

\[
T_x = T_1 \cdot x^{b}
\]

where \( T_x \) is the cost of the \( x \)-th unit, \( T_1 \) is the cost of the first unit, and \( b = \log_2(PR) \) is the learning elasticity. The **progress ratio** \( PR \) is the fraction to which cost falls when cumulative output doubles.

### A.2 Two Parameterizations and Their Relationship

| Quantity | Symbol | Definition | Relationship |
|---|---|---|---|
| Progress ratio | \( PR \) | \( c(2Q)/c(Q) \) | \( PR = 2^{-\alpha} \) |
| Learning rate | \( LR \) | \( 1 - PR \) | \( LR = 1 - 2^{-\alpha} \) |
| Learning exponent | \( \alpha \) | Exponent in \( c(Q) = c_0 (Q/Q_0)^{-\alpha} \) | \( \alpha = -\log_2(PR) = -\ln(PR)/\ln 2 \) |

**Critical correction.** The original proposal conflated the learning rate with the exponent. A 20% cost reduction per doubling means \( PR = 0.80 \), so:

\[
\alpha = -\log_2(0.80) = -\frac{\ln 0.80}{\ln 2} = \frac{0.22314}{0.69315} \approx 0.3219
\]

An exponent of \( \alpha = 0.20 \) would correspond to \( PR = 2^{-0.20} \approx 0.871 \), i.e., a 12.9% learning rate. The baseline in this dissertation is therefore:

\[
\boxed{\alpha = 0.32 \quad (LR \approx 20\%)}
\]

### A.3 Reconciliation with the Google *Joule* Estimate

The Google *Joule* analysis reports "approximately 20% each year" rather than per doubling. If the *annual* reduction is 20%, and cumulative mass doubles every \( \tau \) years, then:

\[
(1 - LR_{\text{annual}})^{\tau} = PR \quad \Rightarrow \quad \tau = \frac{\ln PR}{\ln(1 - LR_{\text{annual}})}
\]

For \( LR_{\text{annual}} = 0.20 \) and \( PR = 0.80 \):

\[
\tau = \frac{\ln 0.80}{\ln 0.80} = 1 \text{ year}
\]

So the two statements are consistent only if cumulative mass doubles annually. The dissertation treats \( \alpha = 0.32 \) as the structural parameter and \( \tau \) (doubling time) as an empirical input to be measured from launch records.

### A.4 Cost Trajectory Scenarios

Using \( c_0 = \$2{,}720/\text{kg} \) (Falcon 9 dedicated-ride reference, 2026), \( Q_0 = 500 \) tonnes cumulative (approx. Falcon-era baseline), and \( \alpha = 0.32 \):

| Scenario | Target cost | Required \( Q/Q_0 \) | Required cumulative mass (t) | Approx. Starship launches at 100 t payload |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| Conservative | $500/kg | \( (2720/500)^{1/0.32} \approx 189 \) | 94,500 | ~950 |
| Baseline | $200/kg | \( (2720/200)^{1/0.32} \approx 2{,}300 \) | 1,150,000 | ~11,500 |
| Aggressive | $100/kg | \( (2720/100)^{1/0.32} \approx 11{,}200 \) | 5,600,000 | ~56,000 |

