## Appendix J — Reproducibility, Ethics, and Data Management

### J.1 Code and Data

- All code in Python 3.12, version-controlled on GitHub, released under MIT license.
- System-dynamics model implemented in `scipy.integrate.solve_ivp`; BN in `pgmpy`.
- Monte Carlo seeds recorded; all figures reproducible from raw data with a single script.
- Data deposited in a Zenodo repository with DOI at time of defense.

### J.2 Expert Elicitation Ethics

- IRB review (exempt category: expert opinion, no personal data beyond professional role).
- Informed consent obtained; experts may withdraw at any time.
- Anonymity preserved in reporting; disagreements reported as aggregates.
- No compensation beyond acknowledgment.

### J.3 Limitations Declared Up Front

1. The learning-curve exponent is estimated from a small number of historical data points; uncertainty is large.
2. The legal multiplier is calibrated from two points in one study; \( L^* \) is an estimand, not a finding.
3. The BN CPTs are elicited, not estimated; backtesting against historical analogs provides calibration evidence, not proof.
4. The model excludes asteroid mining, Mars settlement, and in-space energy export; these are out of scope and treated as boundary conditions.
5. Market-size projections are drawn from commercial reports whose methodologies are not fully public.

### J.4 Pre-Registration

Model-comparison predictions (Appendix F.2) and the primary falsification threshold (\( BF > 10 \)) are pre-registered on OSF before any model fitting. Deviations are reported and justified.

